{
  "description": "two `every 2` handlers are both due on tick 2; the first handler's body runs a nested `wait 2` that advances the clock through the second handler's tick-4 boundary. Claiming the whole due batch up front (marking both handlers `claimed`) means the inner wait cannot claim the already-claimed sibling a second time, so it is not fired out of chronological order: the tick-2 batch runs them once each, in registration order, printing a then b. Under maintainer ruling #984 the tick-4 occurrences that arrived while the batch was in flight are QUEUED rather than dropped (spec/interaction-events.md:189-196), and both are drained after the batch -- again in registration order -- so the measured sequence is a, b, a, b. What this fixture pins is the ORDERING guarantee, and it pins it twice over: a reversed drain, or one that let a sibling's nested wait steal an occurrence the outer batch already owned, would interleave these four prints differently.",
